<Project>

  <!--
    Whether the sibling libraries' DEBUG-only debug-inspector surface is available to compile against.
    The reasoning for needing BOTH conditions is in Directory.Build.props, beside the block this
    replaced; what matters here is WHY it is in .targets rather than .props.

    Directory.Build.props is imported from the top of Microsoft.Common.props, before the SDK defaults
    $(Configuration) to Debug. So `'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'` is a comparison against an EMPTY
    string there for any build that does not pass the configuration explicitly, which is every plain
    `dotnet build` and `dotnet run`. The constant went undefined, the inspector wiring compiled out,
    and nothing said so, because an #if that is merely false is not a diagnostic. Directory.Build.targets
    is imported at the end of Microsoft.Common.targets, after the default has been applied, and
    DefineConstants set here still reaches Csc.

    The rule this is an instance of: NEVER test $(Configuration) (or anything else the SDK defaults)
    from Directory.Build.props. Put it in Directory.Build.targets, where the value is settled.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' AND '$(UseLocalSiblings)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);SIBLING_DEBUG_INSPECTORS</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Licence + attribution text travels with every binary we publish.

    The release assets are native-AOT, so the whole dependency graph is statically linked into one
    executable. AGPL section 4 wants our own licence conveyed with it, MIT/Apache/BSD each want their
    terms and copyright notice reproduced, and the .tar.gz assets carried NONE of it, not even
    LICENSE. Attaching the files here rather than in each of the six csproj files means a seventh
    AOT binary is covered the day it is added, without anyone remembering to.

    Conditioned on PublishAot, which is why this is in .targets and not .props: it is set in the
    csproj body, so a condition on it from .props compares against an empty string. That is the same
    trap the block above documents for $(Configuration), which is worth stating twice because it
    fails silently both times; the item group simply would not apply and nothing would say so.

    CopyToPublishDirectory only, deliberately: a plain `dotnet build` has no redistribution to
    attach notices to, and copying them to bin/ on every build would just be noise in the way.

    astap-readme.txt is here because NOTICE cites it as the place ASTAP's own copyright and licence
    notice is kept verbatim, so shipping NOTICE without it would point at something not present.
    THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt is generated by tools/generate-third-party-notices.ps1.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true'">
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\LICENSE"
             Link="LICENSE" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Visible="false" />
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\LICENSE.EXCEPTION"
             Link="LICENSE.EXCEPTION" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Visible="false" />
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\NOTICE"
             Link="NOTICE" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Visible="false" />
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\FONT-LICENSES.txt"
             Link="FONT-LICENSES.txt" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Visible="false" />
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt"
             Link="THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Visible="false" />
    <Content Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\astap-readme.txt"
             Link="astap-readme.txt" CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
